A||||9||deceler8||Laptop hard drive...need HELP...||||1||
Z||000000||deceler8||06-04-2001||05:54 PM||||I have this Panasonic Pentium laptop I picked up real cheap because it didn't have a hard drive. I have a hard drive that will work, but doesn't have an operating system on it. <P>Is there a way to put Windows 95 on this laptop hard drive using my desktop computer ?<P>My desktop has Windows 95 and it has a hard drive and CD-ROM attached to the same ribbon cable. I have been loaned a laptop hard drive converter and I have another ribbon cable if I need it.<P>How do I go about, first, installing this laptop hard drive, and, second, putting the necessary files on it so it will be bootable on this laptop ?<P>The laptop has a CD ROM but no floppy drive.<P>Since there's no hard drive in the laptop, it won't boot, and since there's no floppy, I can't boot the laptop via this method.<P>There's gotta be a way to do this...any experts wanna take a crack at this ?<P>I'll be forever grateful...really.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||06-04-2001||06:03 PM||||Are you <I>sure</I> it doesn't have a floppy drive???  I've got two laptops (an absolutely ancient one with no CDROM drive but with a floppy drive) and a much newer one with both).  Both of them have floppy drives.<P>What I want to know is how I can exchange the CDROM drive in my 1 year old IBM Thinkpad for a DVDROM drive.  (Gotta be able to watch those movies while I'm on the go!)<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||06-05-2001||09:01 AM||||Yes...I'm sure it doesn't have a floppy...it's a Panasonic CF41 that has a battery that's interchangeable with the floppy (it has the battery but not the floppy).<P>It's a machine that used to be an in-car Highway patrol machine. Pentium 120 mhz, 16 ram. They probably took the hard drive as it might have had secret info on how to defeat their radar or something like that.||||reg||1
Z||000003||WampusCat||06-05-2001||09:07 AM||||With my Dell, I can boot from a floppy hooked to the parallel port on the back of the computer.  Know anybody with an external floppy drive?||||reg||1
Z||000004||thomas_l||06-05-2001||09:12 AM||||The old drive probably got choked with donut crumbs and coffee spillage  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. Two things come to mind. See if the CMOS has a CD boot option. Since its an old one, it may not. The other option would be to scour eBay for a floppy that will work. Check newsgroups and stuff to see who <I>really</I> made that PC and you might find another make/model that will interchange accessories. Last but not least, see if someone local has a laptop to act as a "host" for a DOS boot install and Windows cab files. If you do that you might want to create a 200MB or so partition so you don't need to skip the format step. When it's all done, you could leave it or use Partition Magic to patch things up.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||06-05-2001||11:05 AM||||Well, I'm not sure about the converter and cables you've got, but it sounds like you can hook up the laptop HDD to your home PC.  If so, all you need to do is "format x: /s" the laptop HDD, where x: is the drive letter it gets assigned.  That'll put your operating system on it.  If you don't want to format, you can just "sys x:" to make it bootable.  Then make a directory on the HDD called "CABS" or whatever you want to call it.  If you're using Windows 9x, there should be a directory called CABS on your PC. That or on the Windows CD.  Do a search for *.cab to find it.  Copy the folder with the cab files, and all it's subfolders to the CABS folder on your laptop HDD.  Now, when you install the HDD into the laptop, it'll boot to a c:&gt; prompt.  You can then "cd \cabs" and then run "setup.exe" to start the Windows setup on the laptop.  You will need the OEM number for you OS, or the install won't work.  Let me know if you have any other questions.||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||06-05-2001||05:53 PM||||Sounds easy, but I have more questions...<P>First of all, on my desktop the hard drive and CDROM are connected to the same IDE ribbon. There is another slot on my motherboard that looks as if it will accept another IDE ribbon cable. Is this where I hook up my laptop drive ?<P>And do I need to setup this drive as a slave, and if so, how do I do this...<P>Or does the system auto-detect it somehow ?<P>Should the two hard drives be connected to the same ribbon cable, and if yes, what do I do with the CDROM ? Connect it to the other slot in the motherboard with the other IDE ribbon cable ?<P>Really hoping someone can help.||||reg||1
Z||000007||deceler8||06-06-2001||10:24 AM||||Anyone ?||||reg||1
Z||000008||deceler8||06-06-2001||04:13 PM||||Got it to work. Thanks Formula.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||06-06-2001||10:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by deceler8:<BR><B>Sounds easy, but I have more questions...<P>First of all, on my desktop the hard drive and CDROM are connected to the same IDE ribbon. There is another slot on my motherboard that looks as if it will accept another IDE ribbon cable. Is this where I hook up my laptop drive ?<P>And do I need to setup this drive as a slave, and if so, how do I do this...<P>Or does the system auto-detect it somehow ?<P>Should the two hard drives be connected to the same ribbon cable, and if yes, what do I do with the CDROM ? Connect it to the other slot in the motherboard with the other IDE ribbon cable ?<P>Really hoping someone can help.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry for the delay.  Yeah, it sounds like you have the right idea.  The second IDE slot on the Mobo would be the easiest place to plug in the HDD, cuz you won't have to worry about jumpers.<P>Hope it works ok.  Good luck!||||reg||14
